# Default: OO + SOLID + layered design

Unless the user **explicitly** asks for procedural style (scripts, linear demos,
“keep it flat”, teaching a pre-OO chapter), prefer **full object orientation**,
**SOLID**, and **modular layered** structure.

## Default shape

| Layer | Owns |
| --- | --- |
| Domain | `entity` / `data` / `class` with real types — not bare `dict` / `object` rows |
| Mapping / ports | tuple↔entity, repository/mapper boundaries |
| Application / API | thin handlers; convert domain → DTO/dict only at the edge |
| Infrastructure | DB, HTTP clients, drivers typed (`MySQLCursor`, `ShopDatabase`, …) |

- Prefer named types over `object` (ADR-025: `object` is a **last resort** for true foreign/opaque values).
- Prefer methods and small modules with one reason to change over god-files of free functions that pass untyped bags around.
- Mirror existing layered examples in-repo when present (e.g. teaching `examples/rest-api/shop/mysql` vs lazy `dict` mappers).

## When procedural is OK

Only if the user says so, or the artifact is clearly a one-shot script / early
book chapter that has not taught classes yet. Do not “simplify” a layered
design back into procedural `object`/`dict` glue without that signal.

## Anti-patterns

```text
❌ function dict productRow(object row, object db) { … }
❌ public object cursor() { return this.conn.cursor() }
❌ hasattr(existing, "status_code") to mix entity and HTTP error

✅ Product productFromRow(tuple row, ShopDatabase db)
✅ public MySQLCursor cursor()
✅ nullable<Product> load…; JSONResponse only at the HTTP error edge
```
